!! Highlights

* //Making// is at the centre of both feminism and design.
* Design //makes// our world, and while doing so, it reproduces discriminatory structure.
** because designers can't help but perpetuate the ideas around them? Design's history is rich with drastically changing the culture, with believing in things, with ideologies of freedom and equality... so Design itself isn't really the problem?
*** where is the problem then? society at large? the desire to turn designing into market forces?
* design emerged at the same time as the production of industrial goods in the 19th century, capitalism, and the value system of modernism. All of them are interwoven and developed within patriarchal structures.
* form follows function and [[Less Is More]] are understood as objective... this is western, white, and male. So...
* There is neither neutral knowledge production nor a value-free design practice
* the question is not how we can design without values, but instead which values do we follow
* An examination of one’s positionality and responsibility must be part of design [[Education]].
* Reflective decision-making and action require a conscious approach to norms, the canon and tools – only in this way can possible adverse effects be recognised and prevented.

!! Questions

* Which skin colour, gender, sexual orientation, financial background do I have?
* Where do I stand in the hierarchies of our society?
* Given my professional and personal experience and positionality, am I able to treat the topic in question appropriately and respectfully?
* Are there people more qualified than me?
* How do we define design and which processes and people are included or excluded by this definition?
* What can we learn from the stories behind things?
* How did the design canon come into being?
* Do we need star designers at all (regardless of their identity)?
* Why do we continue to propagate the concept of the lone warrior and ignore the fact that design processes are all collaborative?
* Who develops design tools and based on what assumptions?
